<title>SWEDEN: Incentive Q1 profit falls 29 percent.</title>
<headline>Incentive Q1 profit falls 29 percent.</headline>
<dateline>STOCKHOLM 1997-05-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Three months to March 31, 1997,</p>
<p>			     (million crowns unless stated)</p>
<p>Profit after net financial items</p>
<p> including associates		   828   vs  1,174</p>
<p> Sales				    4,783   vs  5,286</p>
<p> Shr (crowns)			    5.22   vs   7.78</p>
<p> One-off gains			    435   vs    570</p>
<p>Profit after net financial items</p>
<p> excluding associates		   473   vs    826</p>
<p> Operating profit			 678   vs  1,010</p>
<p> Operating costs			4,105   vs  4,276</p>
<p> Net financial items	 CHARGE   205   vs    184   CHARGE</p>
<p> Income from associates		 355   vs    348</p>
<p> Taxes					467   vs    545</p>
<p> Minorities				   4   vs     97</p>
<p> Net profit				 357   vs    532</p>
<p>NOTE - Swedish investment company Incentive AB was expected to post a pre-tax profit of 921 million crowns in the first quarter of 1997, including a capital gain of 425 million crowns from sales of shares in Wabco and Electrolux, a Reuter poll showed.</p>
<p>-- Stockholm newsroom +46-8-7001017</p>
</text>
